  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the 8 files (one dedup) needed to trace this issue:\n\n1. **`werkzeug/src/werkzeug/local.py`** \u2014 `Local`/`LocalStack` classes; shows the 2.3.x implementation backed by `contextvars.ContextVar` (dict/list value), replacing the old threadlocal storage.\n2. **`flask/src/flask/globals.py`** \u2014 defines `_cv_app`/`_cv_request` as module-level `ContextVar`s and the `LocalProxy`-style accessors that back the global `request`/`current_app`/`g`/`session` proxies.\n3. **`flask/src/flask/ctx.py`** \u2014 `RequestContext`/`AppContext` `push()`/`pop()`, which call `ContextVar.set()`/`reset()` (`_cv_tokens`) to bind/unbind context per push.\n4. **`flask/src/flask/app.py`** \u2014 `ensure_sync`/`async_to_sync`, which wraps async view functions via `asgiref.sync.async_to_sync` for each request; this is the boundary where context is captured for the request's own coroutine but not for tasks spawned separately inside it.\n5. **`flask/docs/async-await.rst`** \u2014 \"Background tasks\" section explicitly documents that `asyncio.create_task` spawned inside a Flask async view gets cancelled/loses context when the request's event loop stops, and that context is not guaranteed to propagate outside the single per-request `asgiref` event-loop lifecycle.\n6. **`flask/docs/reqcontext.rst`** \u2014 explains context locals are implemented via `contextvars` and how push/pop scoping works.\n7. **`werkzeug/docs/local.rst`** \u2014 explains `ContextVar.get()` semantics for `Local`/`LocalStack`, including behavior across threads/tasks.\n8. **`werkzeug/CHANGES.rst`** / **`flask/CHANGES.rst`** \u2014 record the exact release where `Local` switched from threadlocal to `ContextVar`-based storage (werkzeug 2.0), which is why the customer's 2.0-vs-2.3 behavior comparison is meaningful.",
